Today we'll learn:

UNION and UNION ALL

These are used to combine results from multiple SELECT queries.

Why Do We Need UNION?

Suppose we have two tables.

Employees_2024
emp_id	name
1	John
2	Jane
Employees_2025
emp_id	name
3	Bob
4	Alice

Now we want:

John
Jane
Bob
Alice

from both tables together.

UNION

Combines results and removes duplicates.

Syntax
SELECT column_name
FROM table1

UNION

SELECT column_name
FROM table2;
Example
SELECT name
FROM Employees_2024

UNION

SELECT name
FROM Employees_2025;
Output
name
John
Jane
Bob
Alice
Important Rule

Both SELECT statements must have:

✅ Same number of columns

✅ Compatible data types

Correct
SELECT name
FROM Table1

UNION

SELECT name
FROM Table2;
Wrong
SELECT name, salary
FROM Table1

UNION

SELECT name
FROM Table2;

❌ Different number of columns.

Understanding Duplicate Removal

Suppose:

Table A
name
John
Jane
Table B
name
Jane
Bob

Query:

SELECT name FROM A

UNION

SELECT name FROM B;
Output
name
John
Jane
Bob

Notice:

Jane appears only once

because UNION removes duplicates.

UNION ALL

Now let's see the difference.

Syntax
SELECT column_name
FROM table1

UNION ALL

SELECT column_name
FROM table2;
Example
SELECT name FROM A

UNION ALL

SELECT name FROM B;
Output
name
John
Jane
Jane
Bob

Notice:

Jane appears twice

because UNION ALL keeps duplicates.

Easy Comparison
Feature	UNION	UNION ALL
Combines results	✅	✅
Removes duplicates	✅	❌
Faster	❌	✅
Why is UNION ALL Faster?
UNION
  ↓
Combine
  ↓
Check duplicates
  ↓
Remove duplicates

Extra work.

UNION ALL
  ↓
Combine
  ↓
Done

No duplicate checking.

Real Interview Question
Difference Between UNION and UNION ALL?

UNION

Removes duplicates.

UNION ALL

Keeps duplicates.
Faster.
Example with Salaries
SELECT salary
FROM Employee

UNION

SELECT salary
FROM Manager;

Returns unique salaries.

SELECT salary
FROM Employee

UNION ALL

SELECT salary
FROM Manager;

Returns all salaries including duplicates.

